  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nToday into Tonight...Light winds, clear skies have produced ideal\nradiational cooling conditions across the region early this \nmorning, with temps dropping into the 30s across the typical cold \nspots across northern WI. A little patchy ground fog will once \nagain be possible early this morning and overnight into early\nTuesday, mainly across central and northern WI. Any fog would \nburn off shortly after sunrise. Otherwise, dry and quiet \nconditions are expected today into tonight, with high clouds \nincreasing through the day. It will be less windy, with northwest \nwinds generally under 12 mph, with some higher gusts this morning \nin/near Door Co.\n\nShower/Storm Chances Tuesday into Wednesday...Chances for showers\nand storms will increase on Tuesday as a shortwave and cold front\napproach from the west. The HRRR is the most aggressive with the \nfirst batch of showers and isolated storms, bringing it all the \nway through the area Tuesday morning. Other CAMs hint at some \nactivity, but thinking the dry air will eat away at its eastward \nadvancement, so have only brought 20-30% chances in the parts of \ncentral and north-central WI. Behind the initial area, instability\nwill build within the warm sector, with SBCAPEs climbing to at \nleast 500-1000 J/kg, highest in north-central WI, with some 2000 \nJ/kg possible towards northwest WI. 0-6 km bulk shear should be in\nthe 25-30 kt range, allowing for some organized storm activity. \nCould have some isolated activity in the warm sector during the \nday, but the main show looks to develop across northwest WI during\nthe afternoon/evening as the shortwave dives into northern MN and\nthe front arrives. Machine Learning guidance has honed in on this\narea for the greatest severe weather threat, which could extend \ninto parts of north central WI Tuesday evening (SPC brings the \nMarginal Risk to the CWA border), with gusty winds and hail the \nmain threats. Storms will likely become more elevated through the \nnight, with the area of showers and embedded shores spreading \nacross the area Tuesday evening/night, with 50-80% chances across \nthe area. While widespread heavy rain is unlikely with probabilities\nof over 0.5\" at 50% or less (highest over northern WI), some \nlocally heavy rain will be possible in any storms, and especially \nif any training storms occur. Other areas of moderate rain are \nalso expected Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, so many spots \nwill at least put a dent into the drought conditions. Models \nremain in fair agreement with faster FROPA on Wednesday, pushing \nthe primary severe risk farther east. Enough cool air aloft with \nthe trough to result in shower/storm chances (20-70%, highest \nnorth/east), especially during peak heating. Lingering instability\ncould lead to a strong storm with gusty winds and hail. \n\nThursday into the Weekend...northwest, cyclonic flow across the \nwestern Great Lakes could produce a few showers on Thursday, \nespecially in the afternoon during peak heating and steepest low-\nlevel lapse rates. But with the colder air aloft and better forcing\nto our north/east, along with dry air, will limit the shower\npotential with chances under 20% (highest near Door Co.). Dry\nconditions are expected on Friday as weak high pressure slides \nacross the region. Next chances for showers and storms (20-40%) \nmove back into the area from the southwest this weekend as flow \naloft becomes more zonal. While the higher threat for severe weather\nand flooding will be to our south/west, any northward shift would\nbring the threat into our area, at least for a day or two. \n\nTemperatures...After the chilly conditions this morning, temps \nthe rest of the week look to be near normal or slightly above \nnormal. Warmest days with highs in the low-mid 80s should be \nTuesday ahead of the front. Warmest night in the 60s much of the \narea will be Tuesday night with clouds, showers/storms and some \nwind. Lows could drop back into the upper 30s and 40s again late \nthis week over the north.",
